This solution visits the pattern cells recursively instead of using nested loops. It moves across one row first. When the row ends, it prints a newline and starts the next row. A cell gets a star when it touches the outside edge.

```javascript
class Solution {
    pattern21(n) {
        const printCell = (row, col) => {
            if (row === n) {
                return;
            }

            const isBorder = row === 0 || row === n - 1 ||
                             col === 0 || col === n - 1;

            process.stdout.write(isBorder ? '*' : ' ');

            if (col === n - 1) {
                process.stdout.write('\n');
                printCell(row + 1, 0);
            } else {
                printCell(row, col + 1);
            }
        };

        if (n > 0) {
            printCell(0, 0);
        }
    }
}
```

The stopping check prevents the recursion from going past the last row. An `n` of `0` produces nothing. There are `n^2` recursive calls, so the time complexity is `O(n^2)` and the recursion stack uses `O(n^2)` space.

Instead of printing one character at a time, this code creates each complete row first. The first and last rows contain only stars. For a middle row, the first and last characters are stars and the part between them is filled with spaces.

```python
class Solution:
    def pattern21(self, n):
        if n <= 0:
            return

        full_row = '*' * n

        for row in range(n):
            if row == 0 or row == n - 1:
                print(full_row)
            else:
                print('*' + ' ' * (n - 2) + '*')
```

An input of `n = 1` prints one star because the first-row case is used. Non-positive values produce no output.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in total because the printed square has `n^2` positions.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the row string.

The border of the square is made of stars. A position is on the border when it is in the first row, last row, first column, or last column. All other positions get a space.

For example, with `n = 4`, the output is:

```text
****
*  *
*  *
****
```

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern21(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
                bool isBorder = row == 0 || row == n - 1 ||
                                col == 0 || col == n - 1;

                cout << (isBorder ? '*' : ' ');
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because every square position is checked.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

Here the whole pattern is stored in a character grid before it is printed. The grid starts with spaces, then the border cells are changed to stars. This makes the border rule easy to see, but it needs more memory than printing directly.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public void pattern21(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        char[,] grid = new char[n, n];

        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
                grid[row, col] = ' ';

                if (row == 0 || row == n - 1 ||
                    col == 0 || col == n - 1) {
                    grid[row, col] = '*';
                }
            }
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
                System.Console.Write(grid[row, col]);
            }
            System.Console.WriteLine();
        }
    }
}
```

For `n = 2`, every cell belongs to the border, so the result is a 2 by 2 block of stars. The grid uses `O(n^2)` space, while the time complexity is `O(n^2)`.

This version checks each position with a small helper method. The helper returns `true` only for cells on the outside of the square, so the inside remains blank.

Dry run for `n = 3`:

- Row `0`: every column is on the border, so it prints `***`.
- Row `1`: only the first and last columns are borders, so it prints `* *`.
- Row `2`: every column is on the border, so it prints `***`.

```java
class Solution {
    private boolean isBorder(int row, int col, int n) {
        return row == 0 || row == n - 1 || col == 0 || col == n - 1;
    }

    public void pattern21(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
                if (isBorder(row, col, n)) {
                    System.out.print('*');
                } else {
                    System.out.print(' ');
                }
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

The loops still visit all `n * n` cells, so the time complexity is `O(n^2)`. The extra space used is `O(1)`.

